    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#juan-bautista-alberdi"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/juan-bautista-alberdi.jpg" alt="Juan Bautista Alberdi" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#juan-bautista-alberdi">Juan Bautista Alberdi</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Juan_Bautista_Alberdi-restored.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">William George Helsby (1828-1891) [1], Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Argentine Republic took its modern form after decades of conflict between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> and the interior provinces that followed independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#spain">Spain</a>, declared at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#congress-of-tucuman">Congress of Tucumán</a> in 1816. In 1853 a constituent congress at Santa Fe adopted a federal constitution inspired partly by that of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#united-states">United States</a> and by the ideas of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#juan-bautista-alberdi">Juan Bautista Alberdi</a>, but the province of Buenos Aires refused to join and existed as a separate State of Buenos Aires, while the other provinces formed the Argentine Confederation under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#justo-jose-de-urquiza">Justo José de Urquiza</a>, with its capital at Paraná. Defeated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-cepeda-1859">Battle of Cepeda</a> in 1859, Buenos Aires accepted the constitution in amended form in 1860, a reform that also confirmed the name Argentine Republic. Renewed conflict ended at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-pavon">Battle of Pavón</a> on 17 September 1861, when Urquiza withdrew his troops from a field he had not clearly lost, leaving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bartolome-mitre">Bartolomé Mitre</a> and the Buenos Aires army in control. The Confederation’s president, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#santiago-derqui">Santiago Derqui</a>, resigned, the provinces delegated national powers to Mitre, and in 1862 he was elected the first president of the reunified republic. Buenos Aires served as the seat of the national government, although its status as federal capital was not settled until 1880.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#buenos-aires"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/buenos-aires.jpg" alt="Buenos Aires" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Puerto_Madero,_Buenos_Aires_(40689219792)_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Deensel, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Between the 1870s and 1914 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#argentina">Argentina</a> became one of the great export economies of the world. British capital financed railways that fanned out from the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> across the pampas, reaching more than 30,000 kilometres by 1914, and land taken from Indigenous peoples was divided into large estates, or <em>estancias</em>, which raised <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cattle">cattle</a> and sheep and grew wheat, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#maize">maize</a> and linseed, often through tenant farmers. Refrigerated shipping, first tried in the late 1870s, opened the British market to Argentine beef. Several million European immigrants arrived, mostly from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#italy">Italy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#spain">Spain</a>, and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#census">census</a> of 1914 about 30 per cent of the population was foreign-born. Buenos Aires grew into one of the largest cities in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#americas">Americas</a> and the birthplace of the tango, while Law 1420 of 1884 made primary education free, compulsory and secular. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#great-depression">Great Depression</a> ended the export model, and from the 1930s, above all under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#juan-peron">Juan Perón</a> after 1946, governments promoted import-substituting industry, nationalised railways and utilities and expanded labour rights and welfare. Since the 1950s growth has been repeatedly interrupted by high <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#inflation">inflation</a>, debt crises and swings between protectionist and market-oriented policies, although agriculture, now led by soybeans, remains the main source of exports.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-curupayty"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-curupayty.jpg" alt="Battle of Curupayty" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-curupayty">Battle of Curupayty</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:C%C3%A1ndido_Lopez_-_Trinchera_de_Curupayt%C3%AD_-_Google_Art_Project_(497159).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Luis Candido Lopez, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The new republic’s army was tested almost at once. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#paraguay">Paraguay</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#francisco-solano-lopez">Francisco Solano López</a> seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#corrientes">Corrientes</a> in 1865, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#argentina">Argentina</a> joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#brazil">Brazil</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#uruguay">Uruguay</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#treaty-of-the-triple-alliance">Treaty of the Triple Alliance</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bartolome-mitre">Mitre</a> commanded the allied armies in the early campaigns, including the costly defeat at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#battle-of-curupayty">Battle of Curupayty</a> in 1866. The war ended in 1870 with Paraguay devastated and Argentina confirmed in possession of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#misiones-province">Misiones</a> and part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#gran-chaco">Chaco</a>. At home the army put down provincial uprisings and then, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#conquest-of-the-desert">Conquest of the Desert</a> led by General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#julio-argentino-roca">Julio Argentino Roca</a> in 1878–1879 and continued into the mid-1880s, used Remington rifles, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#telegraph">telegraph</a> and railways to overrun the lands of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mapuche">Mapuche</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ranquel">Ranquel</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#tehuelche">Tehuelche</a> peoples, killing and displacing thousands and opening <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#patagonia">Patagonia</a> to settlement. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#riccheri-law">Riccheri Law</a> of 1901 introduced conscription, and a naval race with Chile ended with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pactos-de-mayo">Pactos de Mayo</a> of 1902. Argentina remained neutral in both world wars until it declared war on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#germany">Germany</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#japan">Japan</a> in March 1945. The armed forces became arbiters of politics, staging coups in 1930, 1943, 1955, 1962, 1966 and 1976. In April 1982 the junta occupied the Falkland Islands, which Argentina calls the Malvinas; British forces retook them by 14 June, at a cost of 649 Argentine lives, 323 of them on the cruiser <em>General Belgrano</em>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#bartolome-mitre"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bartolome-mitre.jpg" alt="Bartolomé Mitre" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#bartolome-mitre">Bartolomé Mitre</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bartolom%C3%A9_Mitre_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown authorUnknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Constitution of 1853, as amended in 1860, created a federal republic with a president elected indirectly for six years without immediate re-election, a bicameral Congress and a Supreme Court, and allowed the central government to intervene in the provinces. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bartolome-mitre">Mitre</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#domingo-faustino-sarmiento">Domingo Faustino Sarmiento</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nicolas-avellaneda">Nicolás Avellaneda</a> built a national army, courts, schools and customs administration, and after the defeat of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> rising in 1880 the city was federalised as the national capital, the province moving its seat to the new city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#la-plata">La Plata</a>. From 1880 Roca’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#national-autonomist-party">National Autonomist Party</a> dominated a conservative order sustained by limited and often fraudulent elections. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#saenz-pena-law">Sáenz Peña Law</a> of 1912 introduced secret and compulsory male suffrage, and in 1916 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#hipolito-yrigoyen">Hipólito Yrigoyen</a> of the Radical Civic Union won the presidency. The coup of 1930 began a long cycle in which civilian governments alternated with military regimes. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#juan-peron">Perón</a>, elected in 1946 and 1951, built a movement based on the trade unions, gave women the vote in 1947 and rewrote the constitution in 1949, before he was overthrown in 1955 and his movement banned for nearly two decades. The dictatorship of 1976–1983, which called itself the National Reorganisation Process, abducted and killed thousands of people; human rights groups estimate up to 30,000 disappeared. A reform of 1994 introduced direct presidential elections and four-year terms.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#eva-peron"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eva-peron.jpg" alt="Eva Perón" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#eva-peron">Eva Perón</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Evita_(cropped).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Pinélides Fusco, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#argentina">Argentina</a> became the most prosperous and urbanised country in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#latin-america">Latin America</a> in the early twentieth century and, by some estimates, among the ten richest nations in the world by income per head before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#first-world-war">First World War</a>. Its later relative decline from a position comparable to that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#canada">Canada</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#australia">Australia</a> became a classic puzzle of economic history. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#peronism">Peronism</a>, a mass movement combining nationalism, state-led industry and organised labour, shaped populist politics across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#latin">Latin</a> America, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#eva-peron">Eva Perón</a> remains one of the best-known Latin American women of the century. Argentine diplomacy produced the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#drago-doctrine">Drago Doctrine</a> of 1902 against the forcible collection of public debts, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#carlos-saavedra-lamas">Carlos Saavedra Lamas</a> won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nobel-peace-prize">Nobel Peace Prize</a> in 1936 for mediating the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#chaco-war">Chaco War</a>. The country has produced Nobel laureates in science, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#bernardo-houssay">Bernardo Houssay</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#luis-federico-leloir">Luis Federico Leloir</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cesar-milstein">César Milstein</a>, writers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#jorge-luis-borges">Jorge Luis Borges</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#julio-cortazar">Julio Cortázar</a>, and the tango, inscribed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO</a> as intangible cultural heritage in 2009. The Trial of the Juntas in 1985 made Argentina an early example of a new democracy prosecuting a former military regime, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mothers-of-the-plaza-de-mayo">Mothers of the Plaza de Mayo</a> became a worldwide symbol of human rights activism. In 2013 the Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#pope-francis">Jorge Mario Bergoglio</a>, became Pope Francis, the first pope from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#americas">Americas</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Into the Present</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia#raul-alfonsin"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/raul-alfonsin.jpg" alt="Raúl Alfonsín" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia#raul-alfonsin">Raúl Alfonsín</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ra%C3%BAl_Alfons%C3%ADn_con_banda_presidencial_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Presidencia de la Nación Argentina, CC BY 2.5 ar</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Argentine Republic still exists, and its recent history is one of rebuilding democracy after the collapse of the military regime. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#raul-alfonsin">Raúl Alfonsín</a> of the Radical Civic Union took office in December 1983, a commission chaired by the writer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#ernesto-sabato">Ernesto Sabato</a> documented the disappearances in the report <em>Nunca más</em>, and the junta leaders were tried in 1985, although later laws and pardons halted further prosecutions until they were annulled in the 2000s. Hyperinflation forced Alfonsín to hand over power early to the Peronist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#carlos-menem">Carlos Menem</a> in 1989, who pegged the peso to the US dollar in 1991, privatised state companies and joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#brazil">Brazil</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#uruguay">Uruguay</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#paraguay">Paraguay</a> in founding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mercosur">Mercosur</a>. The peg collapsed in the crisis of 2001, when riots brought down President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#fernando-de-la-rua">Fernando de la Rúa</a> and the country defaulted on nearly 100 billion dollars of debt. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#nestor-kirchner">Néstor Kirchner</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#cristina-fernandez-de-kirchner">Cristina Fernández de Kirchner</a> presided over a commodity-led recovery from 2003 to 2015, followed by the centre-right <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#mauricio-macri">Mauricio Macri</a> and the Peronist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#alberto-fernandez">Alberto Fernández</a>. In 2023, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#inflation">inflation</a> above 100 per cent a year, voters elected the libertarian economist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#javier-milei">Javier Milei</a>. Today <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#argentina">Argentina</a> is a federal republic of 23 provinces and the autonomous city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> with about 46 million people, the second-largest economy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#south-america">South America</a>, and it maintains its claim to the Falkland Islands.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Argentine Republic rose faster than almost any other country on European immigration and pampas exports, becoming one of the richest nations by 1914, and its long relative decline, military coups and Peronist mass politics made it a defining case of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia#latin">Latin</a> American instability and democratic renewal.</em></p>

